"We are victims of their strategic decisions": Marionnaud employees go on strike to defend 800 jobs at risk
No fewer than 800 employees out of a total workforce of 2,375—a substantial third of Marionnaud’s French staff—along with more than 100 stores, are now in a precarious position. At the heart of the issue is the acquisition of the French perfumery chain—burdened by 120 million euros in debt and interest—for a symbolic one euro by a French buyer, David Konckier, who has made his intentions clear.
"During the Social and Economic Committee (CSE) meeting, he told us explicitly that he would not keep unprofitable stores and that he intended to cut costs," says Hanane El Kacimi. "He added that he was giving himself six months to take stock," notes the CFDT union representative, who anticipates that the first announcements of job cuts will likely come in early 2027.
Staff representatives have crunched the numbers: "There are dozens of loss-making stores, employing over 500 people, not to mention all the facilities that are dilapidated... That brings the total to 800 jobs at risk," sighs Marilyn Gentil, secretary of the CSE and a representative for the CGT union.
